1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,961
The twenty-ninth of August, 1949.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:07,463
Dawn, on the empty steppe of
north-eastern Kazakhstan.

3
00:00:08,203 --> 00:00:10,318
At seven o'clock in the morning,

4
00:00:10,358 --> 00:00:15,099
a device built around a core of
plutonium sits on top of a steel tower.

5
00:00:15,139 --> 00:00:16,782
Then the sky turns white.

6
00:00:17,522 --> 00:00:20,197
The Soviet Union has the atomic bomb.

7
00:00:20,237 --> 00:00:24,821
Four years after Hiroshima,
America's nuclear monopoly is over.

8
00:00:25,561 --> 00:00:29,129
But the road to this explosion
did not begin in Moscow.

9
00:00:29,169 --> 00:00:33,328
Part of it began more than four
thousand kilometres to the west,

10
00:00:33,368 --> 00:00:35,690
in a quiet valley in Czechoslovakia.

11
00:00:35,730 --> 00:00:40,348
A valley whose uranium would soon be
dug out of the earth by prisoners.

12
00:00:52,887 --> 00:00:53,500
Jáchymov.

13
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:55,676
In German, Sankt Joachimsthal.

14
00:00:55,716 --> 00:01:01,188
A small town in the Ore Mountains, on
the border between Bohemia and Saxony.

15
00:01:01,929 --> 00:01:06,946
Five hundred years ago, it was one of
the richest silver towns in Europe.

16
00:01:06,986 --> 00:01:11,448
Its silver coins, the Joachimsthalers,
gave the world a new word.

17
00:01:11,488 --> 00:01:11,933
Thaler.

18
00:01:11,973 --> 00:01:13,388
And, in time, dollar.

19
00:01:14,127 --> 00:01:16,897
But the miners kept
striking a heavy, black,

20
00:01:16,937 --> 00:01:19,962
useless mineral that
spoiled their silver veins.

21
00:01:20,002 --> 00:01:21,686
They called it pitchblende.

22
00:01:21,726 --> 00:01:23,730
Pech, in German, means bad luck.

23
00:01:24,470 --> 00:01:30,888
In 1898, Marie and Pierre Curie used
waste pitchblende from Joachimsthal to

24
00:01:30,928 --> 00:01:35,021
discover two new elements:
polonium, and radium.

25
00:01:35,061 --> 00:01:37,002
The town became famous.

26
00:01:37,042 --> 00:01:41,049
In 1906, it opened the
world's first radon spa.

27
00:01:41,789 --> 00:01:46,818
Yet for centuries, the miners of
these mountains had been dying young,

28
00:01:46,858 --> 00:01:49,498
of a mysterious disease of the lungs.

29
00:01:49,538 --> 00:01:51,236
Today we know the cause.

30
00:01:51,276 --> 00:01:51,670
Radon.

31
00:01:51,710 --> 00:01:55,146
A radioactive gas that
seeps out of uranium ore.

32
00:01:55,886 --> 00:02:01,312
For a long time, uranium itself
was little more than a by-product,

33
00:02:01,352 --> 00:02:04,460
used to colour glass yellow and green.

34
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:05,702
Then came 1945.

35
00:02:10,842 --> 00:02:13,063
On the sixth of August, 1945,

36
00:02:13,103 --> 00:02:17,352
a single American bomb
destroyed the city of Hiroshima.

37
00:02:17,392 --> 00:02:23,901
In Moscow, Joseph Stalin understood that
the balance of power had changed overnight.

38
00:02:24,641 --> 00:02:29,231
Within two weeks, he put his secret
police chief, Lavrentiy Beria,

39
00:02:29,271 --> 00:02:33,019
in charge of a crash programme
to build a Soviet bomb.

40
00:02:33,059 --> 00:02:37,017
Its scientific director was
the physicist Igor Kurchatov.

41
00:02:37,757 --> 00:02:40,219
Kurchatov had brilliant scientists.

42
00:02:40,259 --> 00:02:46,081
Thanks to spies inside the Manhattan Project,
he had many of the American secrets.

43
00:02:46,121 --> 00:02:48,512
What he did not have, was uranium.

44
00:02:49,251 --> 00:02:53,192
The Soviet Union's own
deposits were small and poor.

45
00:02:53,232 --> 00:02:59,087
The richest mines, in the Belgian Congo
and in Canada, were in Western hands.

46
00:02:59,127 --> 00:03:00,772
In the summer of 1945,

47
00:03:00,812 --> 00:03:05,595
the best-known uranium mine within
Stalin's reach was Jáchymov.

48
00:03:06,335 --> 00:03:09,371
Soviet specialists arrived within months.

49
00:03:09,411 --> 00:03:12,521
And on the twenty-third of November, 1945,

50
00:03:12,561 --> 00:03:17,247
Czechoslovakia signed a secret
agreement with the Soviet Union.

51
00:03:17,287 --> 00:03:22,273
Uranium ore mined in Czechoslovakia
would be delivered to the USSR,

52
00:03:22,313 --> 00:03:24,748
at prices below the world market.

53
00:03:25,489 --> 00:03:28,014
Czechoslovakia was still a democracy.

54
00:03:28,054 --> 00:03:30,996
But its uranium already
belonged to Stalin.

55
00:03:36,135 --> 00:03:40,215
The mines became a national
enterprise, Jáchymov Mines.

56
00:03:40,255 --> 00:03:44,260
But real power sat with Soviet
officers and engineers.

57
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:49,578
Uranium became a state secret, and
the whole area became a closed zone.

58
00:03:50,318 --> 00:03:54,486
The first workforce was made
up of German prisoners of war,

59
00:03:54,526 --> 00:03:58,836
alongside Czech miners lured by
high wages and extra rations.

60
00:03:58,876 --> 00:04:03,115
The ore was loaded into sealed
railway wagons and sent east.

61
00:04:03,155 --> 00:04:05,254
Dozens of wagons, every month.

62
00:04:05,994 --> 00:04:12,348
In 1946, Czechoslovakia delivered
eighteen tonnes of uranium.

63
00:04:12,388 --> 00:04:14,444
In 1947, forty-nine.

64
00:04:14,484 --> 00:04:17,694
In 1948, more than one hundred.

65
00:04:17,734 --> 00:04:21,677
In 1949, nearly one hundred and fifty.

66
00:04:22,417 --> 00:04:24,402
It was not the only source.

67
00:04:24,442 --> 00:04:30,027
In defeated Germany, Soviet teams seized
around a hundred tonnes of uranium

68
00:04:30,067 --> 00:04:30,777
compounds.

69
00:04:30,817 --> 00:04:34,751
Soon, the mines of Saxony,
just across the mountains,

70
00:04:34,791 --> 00:04:37,076
would far out-produce Jáchymov.

71
00:04:37,116 --> 00:04:39,401
Bulgaria and Poland added more.

72
00:04:40,141 --> 00:04:45,093
But in the critical early years, when
the Soviet Union had almost nothing,

73
00:04:45,133 --> 00:04:49,410
Jáchymov was one of the very first
foreign sources it could tap.

74
00:04:49,450 --> 00:04:54,739
Historians still debate how much Czechoslovak
uranium ended up inside the first

75
00:04:54,779 --> 00:04:55,076
bomb.

76
00:04:55,116 --> 00:05:00,607
What is certain is that Czechoslovakia
supplied the Soviet programme from its very

77
00:05:00,647 --> 00:05:01,349
first year.

78
00:05:02,089 --> 00:05:06,811
On the twenty-ninth of August,
1949, the programme succeeded.

79
00:05:06,851 --> 00:05:12,510
RDS-1 exploded with the force of about
twenty-two thousand tonnes of TNT.

80
00:05:12,550 --> 00:05:14,461
The West called it Joe-1.

81
00:05:14,501 --> 00:05:18,052
In September, President
Truman told the world.

82
00:05:18,092 --> 00:05:19,926
The arms race had begun.

83
00:05:25,066 --> 00:05:28,490
By then, Czechoslovakia
itself had changed.

84
00:05:28,530 --> 00:05:35,095
In February 1948, the Communist Party, led
by Klement Gottwald, seized full power.

85
00:05:35,835 --> 00:05:40,948
That autumn, a new law allowed people
to be sent to forced labour camps by an

86
00:05:40,988 --> 00:05:43,960
administrative commission,
without any trial.

87
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,038
Mass arrests, purges and
show trials followed.

88
00:05:47,778 --> 00:05:48,990
Priests and monks.

89
00:05:49,030 --> 00:05:52,119
Farmers who refused to
join collective farms.

90
00:05:52,159 --> 00:05:57,682
Students, scouts, and former soldiers who
had fought against Hitler in the West.

91
00:05:57,722 --> 00:06:00,325
Anyone accused of anti-state activity.

92
00:06:01,065 --> 00:06:03,991
The first Soviet bomb
had already been built.

93
00:06:04,031 --> 00:06:07,945
Now Moscow wanted uranium for
the arsenal that would follow.

94
00:06:07,985 --> 00:06:12,097
And the Czechoslovak regime found
its workforce in the prisons.

95
00:06:17,237 --> 00:06:22,262
Eighteen labour camps were built
around the uranium mines of Jáchymov,

96
00:06:22,302 --> 00:06:24,144
Horní Slavkov and Příbram.

97
00:06:24,184 --> 00:06:29,499
Several bore the names of their mines,
which now sounded like cruel jokes.

98
00:06:29,539 --> 00:06:30,150
Equality.

99
00:06:30,190 --> 00:06:30,729
Concord.

100
00:06:30,769 --> 00:06:31,597
Brotherhood.

101
00:06:32,337 --> 00:06:34,053
Between 1949 and 1961,

102
00:06:34,093 --> 00:06:40,439
historians estimate that some sixty-five
thousand prisoners passed through these

103
00:06:40,479 --> 00:06:40,918
camps.

104
00:06:40,958 --> 00:06:44,670
A large share of them
were political prisoners.

105
00:06:45,410 --> 00:06:47,466
They called themselves mukl.

106
00:06:47,506 --> 00:06:52,031
A word often explained as an
acronym: muž určený k likvidaci.

107
00:06:52,071 --> 00:06:54,351
A man destined for liquidation.

108
00:06:55,091 --> 00:06:57,797
Prisoners worked in shifts, day and night,

109
00:06:57,837 --> 00:07:00,608
in tunnels hundreds of
metres below ground.

110
00:07:00,648 --> 00:07:02,177
Every shift had a quota.

111
00:07:02,217 --> 00:07:05,184
Fail to meet it, and your
food ration was cut.

112
00:07:05,925 --> 00:07:08,536
Drilling threw up clouds of rock dust.

113
00:07:08,576 --> 00:07:11,188
The air underground was full of radon.

114
00:07:11,228 --> 00:07:15,235
There were no respirators, no
dosimeters, and no warnings.

115
00:07:15,275 --> 00:07:16,700
The danger was known.

116
00:07:16,740 --> 00:07:18,514
But production came first.

117
00:07:19,254 --> 00:07:20,661
At the Svornost camp,

118
00:07:20,701 --> 00:07:25,072
prisoners climbed a steep stairway
to the mine every single day.

119
00:07:25,112 --> 00:07:29,966
They named it after the Nazi
concentration camp: the Mauthausen stairs.

120
00:07:30,706 --> 00:07:35,290
Punishment meant the correction cell:
cold concrete, and half rations.

121
00:07:35,330 --> 00:07:38,857
Escape attempts ended under
fire from the watchtowers.

122
00:07:38,897 --> 00:07:42,424
Men died in accidents, from
beatings and from bullets.

123
00:07:42,464 --> 00:07:47,775
Many more died years later, of cancers
caused by the radiation they had breathed.

124
00:07:52,915 --> 00:07:56,522
The most feared place of all
stood near the town of Ostrov.

125
00:07:56,562 --> 00:08:00,294
In a brick building that prisoners
called the Tower of Death,

126
00:08:00,334 --> 00:08:04,992
uranium ore was crushed and sorted before
being shipped to the Soviet Union.

127
00:08:05,733 --> 00:08:10,842
Around three hundred prisoners, men the
regime considered especially dangerous,

128
00:08:10,882 --> 00:08:13,710
worked here without any
protective clothing.

129
00:08:13,750 --> 00:08:18,207
They hauled the ore to the top floor,
and tipped it onto huge sieves,

130
00:08:18,247 --> 00:08:21,010
breathing radioactive
dust with every load.

131
00:08:21,750 --> 00:08:26,041
Today, the Red Tower of Death is
a national cultural monument.

132
00:08:26,081 --> 00:08:30,862
One of the few surviving buildings
of the Czechoslovak uranium gulag.

133
00:08:36,002 --> 00:08:41,444
In central Bohemia, near the town of
Příbram, the camp of Vojna still stands.

134
00:08:41,484 --> 00:08:45,930
Barracks, watchtowers and barbed
wire, preserved as a memorial.

135
00:08:45,970 --> 00:08:49,490
Its prisoners worked in
the Příbram uranium mines,

136
00:08:49,530 --> 00:08:52,623
one of the richest
uranium fields in Europe.

137
00:08:57,763 --> 00:09:03,029
In 1960, an amnesty released
many of the political prisoners.

138
00:09:03,069 --> 00:09:06,422
By 1961, the uranium camps were closed.

139
00:09:06,462 --> 00:09:10,075
The mining went on, now with paid workers.

140
00:09:10,815 --> 00:09:12,556
Between 1946 and 1990,

141
00:09:12,596 --> 00:09:18,142
Czechoslovakia mined close to one
hundred thousand tonnes of uranium,

142
00:09:18,182 --> 00:09:20,732
much of it for the Soviet Union.

143
00:09:20,772 --> 00:09:26,156
It became one of the largest uranium
producers in the Eastern Bloc.

144
00:09:26,897 --> 00:09:30,252
For decades, the camps
were a taboo subject.

145
00:09:30,292 --> 00:09:35,575
Only after the fall of communism in
1989 were the political prisoners

146
00:09:35,615 --> 00:09:39,124
rehabilitated, and free
to tell their stories.

147
00:09:39,863 --> 00:09:45,080
Today, a memorial trail called Jáchymov
Hell leads past the ruins of the camps.

148
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:49,871
Every May, former prisoners and their
families gather there to remember.

149
00:09:50,611 --> 00:09:55,939
The Soviet bomb was born from physics,
espionage, and the will of a dictator.

150
00:09:55,979 --> 00:09:59,216
But the Soviet arsenal
was also built on stone.

151
00:09:59,256 --> 00:10:04,793
Stone hauled out of the earth by people
who never chose to take part in the arms

152
00:10:04,833 --> 00:10:05,142
race.

153
00:10:05,882 --> 00:10:08,641
In the valley that gave
the world the dollar,

154
00:10:08,681 --> 00:10:11,751
the true price of uranium
was paid in human lives.

